(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["physiology-exam-3-immunology-quiz-version-3"]={"title":"Blood & Immunology — Quiz 2 (30 Questions)","category":"Physiology","exam":"Exam 3","sem":"summer-1-2026","questions":[{"q":"During acute infection, why do neutrophils dominate the earliest response while macrophages dominate later?","choices":["Neutrophils recirculate for weeks, so they reach tissue before slower monocytes","Macrophages act only after antibodies have coated the pathogen for ingestion","Macrophages must first divide within the tissue before they can phagocytize","Neutrophils are mature and arrive within an hour; macrophages take days to build up"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: mature neutrophils respond immediately, whereas monocytes must accumulate and mature into macrophages over days. — Blood & Immunology, slides 28-30"},{"q":"To reach an infected tissue, white blood cells first leave the capillary by...","choices":["being carried passively into the tissue by bulk fluid flow in the body","squeezing between endothelial cells in a process called diapedesis","converting into red blood cells that diffuse across the wall","dissolving the entire capillary wall with digestive enzymes"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Leukocytes exit the bloodstream by diapedesis — deforming and slipping between the endothelial cells of the capillary or venule wall — and then move toward the infection by chemotaxis, following chemical gradients released at the site. — Blood & Immunology, slides 26-28"},{"q":"Circulating monocytes contribute to defense by...","choices":["producing antibodies against specific bacterial antigens","forming the fibrin meshwork that walls off infection","carrying dissolved oxygen to the sites of active infection in the tissues","entering tissues and maturing into long-lived phagocytic macrophages"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Monocytes circulate briefly, then migrate into tissues where they enlarge and mature into macrophages — powerful, long-lived phagocytes that can ingest many organisms, clear debris, and present antigen to lymphocytes. — Blood & Immunology, slides 13, 17, 30"},{"q":"Basophils and mast cells release histamine and heparin, which contribute to...","choices":["the direct killing of virus-infected cells","the transport of oxygen within the blood","the synthesis of new antibodies in lymph nodes","the inflammatory and allergic response"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Basophils and their tissue counterparts, mast cells, store histamine, heparin, and other mediators; when triggered (for example, by IgE cross-linking), they degranulate, driving the vasodilation, increased permeability, and other features of inflammation and immediate allergy. — Blood & Immunology, slides 34, 64-65"},{"q":"A rise in the circulating white blood cell count (leukocytosis) commonly indicates...","choices":["an active infection or inflammatory process in the body","a fall in the number of red blood cells present as a rule","an excess of platelets circulating in the blood","a reduced ability of the body to fight infection"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Leukocytosis, an increase in circulating white cells, is a typical response to infection or inflammation, as the marrow releases and produces more leukocytes to meet the demand; the pattern of which cells rise can hint at the cause. — Blood & Immunology, slides 26-30"},{"q":"Macrophages play a dual role in immunity because they both...","choices":["form blood clots and also filter lymph in the nodes","phagocytize pathogens and present their antigens to activate lymphocytes","secrete acid and also cross-link fibrin strands during this particular process","produce antibodies and also carry oxygen to tissues"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Macrophages are powerful phagocytes that engulf and destroy pathogens, and they also display fragments of the ingested antigen on MHC to helper T-cells, bridging the innate and adaptive responses — a key dual function. — Blood & Immunology, slides 29-31, 55"},{"q":"A lymph node contributes to immunity by...","choices":["filtering lymph and trapping antigens for presentation to lymphocytes","manufacturing red blood cells for the circulation","producing digestive enzymes for the intestine","storing bile between meals for fat digestion during this particular process"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. As lymph percolates through a node, macrophages and dendritic cells trap and present antigens to the resident lymphocytes; this filtering role brings antigen and responding cells together, which is why nodes enlarge during nearby infections. — Blood & Immunology, slides 22-24"},{"q":"Because lymphatics return interstitial fluid to the blood, blockage of lymphatic drainage typically causes...","choices":["an immediate rise in the arterial blood pressure","a rapid fall in the volume of interstitial fluid","accumulation of fluid in the tissues, producing edema","complete loss of the body's ability to make antibodies"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The lymphatic system continuously returns filtered interstitial fluid (and protein) to the bloodstream; if drainage is blocked, that fluid accumulates in the tissue, causing swelling (edema). This links lymphatic function to fluid balance as well as immunity. — Blood & Immunology, slides 22-24"},{"q":"Lymphocytes are unusual among leukocytes because they...","choices":["survive only a few hours in the blood before being routinely replaced","remain fixed in one tissue for their entire lifespan","recirculate continuously between blood, lymph, and lymphoid tissue","are unable to leave the bloodstream at any time"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Unlike short-lived neutrophils, lymphocytes are long-lived and recirculate endlessly — from blood into lymphoid tissue, into lymph, and back to blood — which maximizes their chance of encountering the specific antigen they recognize. — Blood & Immunology, slide 12"},{"q":"B-lymphocytes complete their maturation in the...","choices":["spleen, which is their only site of origin","thymus, alongside developing T-lymphocytes","liver, throughout the whole of adult life in the body","bone marrow, before circulating to lymphoid tissues"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. In mammals, B-cells mature in the bone marrow (the 'B' is often associated with the bursa in birds, but marrow in humans); once mature, they seed lymph nodes, spleen, and other lymphoid tissue where they can respond to antigen. — Blood & Immunology, slides 40-44"},{"q":"The spleen supports immunity mainly by...","choices":["filtering blood and removing pathogens and worn-out cells","producing digestive bile that destroys the ingested bacteria","secreting digestive enzymes into the bloodstream","manufacturing intrinsic factor needed for B12 uptake"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The spleen filters the blood: macrophages in its red pulp and sinuses remove circulating microbes, debris, and senescent red cells and platelets, while its white pulp houses lymphocytes that mount adaptive responses to blood-borne antigens. — Blood & Immunology, slide 24"},{"q":"Which describes a direct way antibodies neutralize a bacterial toxin?","choices":["Inserting pore-forming perforins directly into the toxin molecule","Binding and covering the toxic site so it cannot act on host cells","Secreting interleukins that suppress further toxin production","Phagocytizing the toxin and digesting it within a phagolysosome"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: neutralization occurs when antibodies cover the toxic/active site of the antigen so it cannot bind host tissue. — Blood & Immunology, slide 52"},{"q":"The complement system enhances immunity primarily by...","choices":["opsonizing microbes, driving inflammation, and lysing target membranes","generating antigen-specific antibodies tailored to each pathogen","producing the memory cells responsible for a faster secondary response","presenting antigen fragments to helper T-cells on MHC molecules"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: complement complements antibody and phagocyte action through opsonization, inflammation, and membrane lysis. — Blood & Immunology, slide 53"},{"q":"Interferons contribute to innate defense against viruses by...","choices":["signaling neighboring cells to resist viral replication","directly binding and neutralizing circulating antibodies","forming the membrane attack complex that lyses cells","carrying oxygen to virus-infected tissues"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Virus-infected cells release interferons, which act on nearby cells to induce an antiviral state — producing proteins that interfere with viral replication — thereby limiting the spread of the virus before the adaptive response is fully mobilized. — Blood & Immunology, slides 52-53, 60"},{"q":"A typical antibody molecule is Y-shaped, and its two identical tips are important because they...","choices":["digest the antigen the way an enzyme would","anchor the antibody permanently to the plasma cell","bind the specific antigen, giving each antibody two antigen-binding sites","carry oxygen to the site of the infection during this process in the tissues"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The variable regions at the two tips of the Y form the antigen-binding sites, both specific for the same antigen; having two sites lets a single antibody link antigens together (aiding agglutination) while the stem region interacts with other immune components. — Blood & Immunology, slides 50-53"},{"q":"Long-lasting immunity after an infection depends on...","choices":["antibodies from the first infection remaining in the blood forever","a permanent rise in body temperature that deters the pathogen","memory lymphocytes that persist and respond rapidly on re-exposure","neutrophils that stay elevated permanently after the infection"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. During a response, some activated B- and T-cells become long-lived memory cells; on later exposure to the same antigen they mount a faster, stronger secondary response, which is the basis of durable immunity and of vaccination. — Blood & Immunology, slides 45-48"},{"q":"Antibodies help neutralize threats directly by...","choices":["engulfing and digesting bacteria the way phagocytes do","synthesizing new complement proteins at the infection site","forming pores that lyse the host's own infected cells in the body's tissues","binding and inactivating toxins or blocking a microbe's ability to attach"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Beyond tagging pathogens for other defenses, antibodies can directly neutralize by binding toxins to render them harmless and by coating microbes to block the sites they use to attach to and enter host cells. — Blood & Immunology, slides 50-53"},{"q":"The redness and warmth of acute inflammation result largely from...","choices":["loss of all blood flow entirely to the damaged tissue region","vasoconstriction that traps blood in the tissue","vasodilation and increased blood flow to the injured area","antibody deposition occurring within the vessel walls"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Inflammatory mediators such as histamine cause local vasodilation and increased blood flow, which produce the redness and warmth; increased capillary permeability then adds the swelling characteristic of inflammation. — Blood & Immunology, slide 25"},{"q":"In immunology, an antigen is best defined as...","choices":["an antibody produced by an activated plasma cell","a signaling chemical that raises the body's temperature","a molecule the immune system recognizes as foreign and responds to","a phagocytic white cell that engulfs bacteria under most normal conditions"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. An antigen is any molecule (often a protein or polysaccharide) that the immune system can recognize as foreign and mount a response against; the specific portion actually bound by an antibody or receptor is called the epitope. — Blood & Immunology, slides 45-53"},{"q":"Why can a cytotoxic T-cell not respond to free, intact antigen the way a B-cell can?","choices":["T-cells must first be opsonized by complement before they can bind antigen","T-cells recognize only antigen fragments displayed on MHC molecules","T-cells lack surface receptors and rely entirely on secreted antibody","T-cells respond only to polysaccharide antigens, never to proteins"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: unlike B-cells, T-cells recognize processed antigen fragments presented by MHC, not free intact antigen. — Blood & Immunology, slide 55"},{"q":"The swelling of acutely inflamed tissue is caused largely by histamine, which...","choices":["dilates local vessels and increases their permeability, letting fluid leak into tissue","forms fibrin that physically enlarges the tissue","carries extra oxygen that expands the tissue during this particular process in the tissues","constricts vessels and drives fluid back into the blood"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Histamine released by mast cells and basophils dilates nearby vessels and increases capillary permeability; plasma fluid then leaks into the tissue, producing the swelling (edema) of acute inflammation along with redness and warmth. — Blood & Immunology, slides 25, 64-65"},{"q":"When activated by their specific antigen, B-lymphocytes proliferate and differentiate into...","choices":["phagocytes that engulf and digest the antigen","cytotoxic cells that directly lyse infected host cells","plasma cells that secrete large amounts of antibody","red blood cells that transport the antibody in plasma"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Antigen-activated B-cells (with helper T-cell support) proliferate and differentiate into plasma cells, which are antibody factories secreting large quantities of the specific antibody, while some become memory B-cells for future responses. — Blood & Immunology, slides 44-50"},{"q":"The primary (first) response to a new antigen is relatively slow because...","choices":["the few matching lymphocytes must be selected and expanded before antibody rises","the innate barriers must fail completely first","memory cells from a prior exposure block the response","antibodies from birth must first be used up during this particular process in the tissues"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. On first exposure, only a small number of lymphocytes recognize the antigen; they must be activated and proliferate into a large clone (and differentiate into plasma cells) before substantial antibody appears, producing a lag of several days in the primary response. — Blood & Immunology, slides 45-48"},{"q":"Humoral immunity and cell-mediated immunity differ chiefly in that humoral immunity...","choices":["uses T-cells directly, while cell-mediated immunity uses antibodies","works through antibodies in body fluids, while cell-mediated immunity uses T-cells directly","provides only immediate barriers such as skin and mucus during this particular process in the tissues","is part of the innate system, while cell-mediated is acquired"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Humoral (antibody-mediated) immunity relies on antibodies secreted by plasma cells that circulate in body fluids to target extracellular threats, whereas cell-mediated immunity uses T-cells (especially cytotoxic T-cells) to act against intracellular pathogens and abnormal cells. — Blood & Immunology, slides 44-59"},{"q":"The enormous variety of antibody specificities is generated by...","choices":["random recombination of gene segments, producing many distinct lymphocyte clones","the antigen itself instructing the lymphocyte how to fold its antibody","the thymus copying antibodies from previously infected individuals","a single gene that mutates anew during each and every separate infection encountered"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A limited set of gene segments (cassettes) recombine in many permutations during lymphocyte development, so millions of clones each bearing a unique specificity arise from a modest number of genes. — Blood & Immunology, slide 44"},{"q":"Humoral and cell-mediated immunity differ in that humoral immunity...","choices":["provides no memory, while cell-mediated generates lasting protection","acts only inside infected cells, while cell-mediated acts in the blood","requires no prior antigen exposure, while cell-mediated must be triggered","uses B-cell-derived antibodies, while cell-mediated uses activated T-cells"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: humoral immunity works through B-cell antibodies; cell-mediated immunity works through activated T-lymphocytes. — Blood & Immunology, slide 37"},{"q":"Natural killer (NK) cells are considered part of innate immunity because they...","choices":["can only act after memory cells have formed during this process in the tissues","destroy virus-infected and abnormal cells without needing prior sensitization","mature in the thymus and require MHC-matched antigen","produce highly specific antibodies after being primed"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. NK cells recognize and kill virus-infected and tumor cells based on general 'danger' signals and altered surface markers, acting quickly without the antigen-specific priming that adaptive lymphocytes require — which places them in the innate arm. — Blood & Immunology, slides 33, 59-60"},{"q":"Passive immunity, such as antibodies passed in breast milk, is limited because...","choices":["it triggers lifelong immunity through the recipient's own T-cells","it works only against the recipient's own tissues rather than against pathogens","it strengthens over time as the antibodies multiply","the recipient makes no memory cells, so protection fades as antibodies degrade"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. In passive immunity the recipient receives ready-made antibodies but mounts no response of their own, so no memory cells form; as the transferred antibodies are gradually degraded, protection wanes within weeks. — Blood & Immunology, slide 63"},{"q":"Self versus non-self recognition by T-cells relies on...","choices":["antibodies coating every one of the body's own cells","MHC molecules that display cell peptides for T-cell inspection","the color of the surface pigments on each host cell","histamine tags that mark self cells as friendly under these conditions"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Major histocompatibility complex (MHC) molecules on cell surfaces present peptide fragments; T-cells inspect these displays and react to foreign peptides while tolerating self, which is how the adaptive system distinguishes self from non-self. — Blood & Immunology, slides 55-58"},{"q":"Immunoglobulin A (IgA) is particularly important because it...","choices":["is the first antibody produced during a new infection during this particular process","protects mucosal surfaces and is secreted in fluids such as saliva and breast milk","forms the membrane attack complex that lyses microbes","is the antibody chiefly responsible for allergic reactions"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. IgA is the main antibody found in secretions — saliva, tears, respiratory and intestinal mucus, and breast milk — where it guards the mucosal surfaces that are common entry points for pathogens and provides passive protection to breastfeeding infants. — Blood & Immunology, slides 50-53"}]};
